## 2. The gap

**Which states have the fewest hospitals per person?**

**Per 100,000 people** means: if the state had 100,000 people, how many hospitals would they share. I
counted secondary and tertiary facilities (general hospitals and above), where specialist care can sit.

![Facilities per 100,000 people by state](exports/chart1_facilities_per_100k.png)

The national figure is **{fac_national}** per 100,000. {fac_high_state} has {fac_high}; {fac_low_state} has
{fac_low}, about {fac_ratio} times fewer. The five lowest are {fac_lowest5}. {halima_state} has {h_fac}.

For dialysis specifically, my hand-built register found a public unit reported working in {k_public_yes}
of {n_units} states. In {k_public_no}, the only reports say it isn't working, and in {k_public_unknown} there
is no evidence either way. *No evidence* is not the same as *no unit*: it means nobody I could find has written
it down.

For a decision-maker, the point is that the gap is concentrated. {low10_north} states with the fewest hospitals per
person are in the three northern zones. The same money buys very different coverage depending
on where it goes.

> **Under the hood.** GRID3 calls its facility list *non-exhaustive and non-validated*: it can miss,
> duplicate or keep closed facilities. I dropped {grid3_dupes} duplicates (same name and area, under {dedup_m} m
> apart). Against a second list (eHealth Africa), {crosscheck_flags} states differ by more than {crosscheck_pct}%.
> Population figures are modelled (Nigeria has had no census since {census_year}), so I round rates to one decimal place.

