## 3. The cause

**Is the gap equipment, workforce or systems?**

This is the question that matters most for anyone trying to fix it, and the one the data answers least.
For each fact in the register I only recorded a cause if the source states one:

- **equipment**: a broken or missing machine
- **workforce**: no nephrologist (kidney specialist) or trained dialysis nurse
- **systems**: power, water treatment, maintenance, consumables supply, referral or payment problems

![Kidney register facts per state, coloured by gap type](exports/chart4_register_gap_types.png)

Of {k_state_facts} state-level kidney facts, only **{k_known}** say what the gap is. Among those, systems
problems come up {k_systems} times, equipment {k_equipment} times and workforce {k_workforce} times. In
{halima_state}, the teaching hospital's unit runs on a standby generator, and its manager put the workforce
problem plainly: "{h_quote}" ({h_quote_src}).

Cancer care looks similar. Of {c_state_facts} state-level radiotherapy facts, {c_known} give a cause: systems
{c_systems}, equipment {c_equipment}, workforce {c_workforce}.

What this means: buying a machine fixes one of three problems. A unit also needs steady power, clean water,
a supply chain for consumables, maintenance and trained staff, and the public evidence rarely says which one
is missing in a given place. That has to be checked on the ground, unit by unit.

> **Under the hood.** "Unknown" means the source doesn't say, not that nothing is wrong. {k_unknown} of
> {k_rows} kidney rows and {c_unknown} of {c_rows} cancer rows have no stated cause. {k_news} of the kidney
> rows come from news reports, labelled as such. {n_checked_ok} of {n_checked} quotes spot-checked against
> their pages matched.

