## 4. Who pays

**Where need is high, can people pay?**

**Out-of-pocket** means paid by the patient or family at the point of care, not through insurance or the
government. Published estimates of Nigeria's out-of-pocket share of health spending run from **{oop_min} to
{oop_max}** ({oop_n} figures, different years and methods; all on the Under the hood page). For dialysis, a
{dialysis_oop_year} survey found **{dialysis_oop}** paid out of pocket. Only {insured_nat} of adults aged 15 to 49 had any health
insurance in the NDHS 2024 survey.

I built a state scorecard with three parts: **need** (how many people live there: a stand-in until the number
of people aged 40 and over, the age group most at risk of kidney disease, is published by state), **supply gap**
(fewer hospitals per person = bigger gap) and **ability to pay** (insurance and household wealth). Each part
is a percentile rank from 0 to 100, so one extreme state can't squash the rest.

I expected the states with the greatest need to have the least ability to pay. {need_pay_finding}

![Need against ability to pay, one dot per state](exports/chart2_need_vs_ability_to_pay.png)

I also tried adding self-reported high blood pressure and blood sugar to need. With them, the link rises to
**{r_need_pay_dx}**. I left them out of the main score on purpose: the survey asks whether a health worker ever
*told* you that you have the condition, so it partly measures who gets tested, and testing rises with wealth.
Including them would tie need to ability to pay by construction.

What holds in both versions is the supply side: {gap_pay_finding} (rank correlation **{r_gap_pay}**).
{orange_corner} states sit in the high-need, low-ability-to-pay corner: {corner_states}. {halima_corner}

That is the tension that makes public-private partnerships (PPPs) relevant: a **PPP** is an arrangement where
a private partner and a public hospital share the investment, the work and the money. Where patients can't
pay the full price, someone else has to: insurance, a subsidy or a long-term investor.

Population matters as well as how need is defined, because need is measured by it. Re-running the scorecard
with the National Population Commission's {npc_year} projection instead of GRID3 (for need and for hospitals per
100,000 people), {n_top10_both_pop} of the top 10 stay in the top 10 under both: {top10_both_pop}. Ogun's place
depends on the source: {ogun_score_rank_grid3} with GRID3, {ogun_score_rank_npc} with the NPC projection.

With equal weights, the top five states are {top5}; the bottom five are {bottom5}. I re-ran the score
{n_weightings} times with different weights, and {n_robust} states stay in the top 10 in at least {robust_pct}% of runs:
{robust_states}.

> **Under the hood.** A screening tool, not investment advice. The out-of-pocket share is national only; I
> never spread it across states. NDHS state figures are survey estimates with margins of error. With
> self-reported diagnoses added to need, {need_dx_overlap} of the top 10 stay the same and {orange_corner_dx} states
> sit in the high-need, low-ability-to-pay corner (both versions are on the app's Under the hood page).

