## 5. What a centre could do

**What would one dialysis centre in a high-gap state deliver?**

I modelled one illustrative centre inside a public hospital: {k_machines} machines, two shifts, six days a
week. **Capacity used** is the share of available session slots actually filled. The **payer mix** is who
pays for each session (family, subsidy, insurance, employer).

![Centre model: patients treated and break-even, low / base / high and with a weaker naira](exports/chart6_centre_model_scenarios.png)

In the base case it treats **{k_patients} people** ({k_sessions} sessions a year) at {k_used} capacity used.
But the money doesn't add up at public prices. It receives about **{k_price}** a session on average and needs
**{k_breakeven}** to break even. Consumables, power and water alone cost {k_variable} a session, because
dialysers and bloodlines are imported and priced in dollars ({k_fx} naira per US$, World Bank {k_fx_year}).
It {k_result} a year. Only the optimistic scenario pays back its {k_capex} set-up cost, in about
{k_high_payback} years, and a naira {shock_a} weaker stretches that to {k_high20_payback}.

I also tested two general PPP structures (illustrative only, not any real organisation's terms): a fee per
session paid to the partner, or a revenue share. They move the shortfall between the partner (about
{ppp_partner} a year) and the hospital ({ppp_hospital}); they don't remove it. Closing the gap needs a higher
tariff, a larger insured or subsidised share at a fuller price, cheaper supplies, or outside funding.

**And cancer care?** One radiotherapy centre with a single **linac** (linear accelerator, the machine that
delivers radiation) could treat about **{c_patients} patients a year** ({c_fractions} daily treatments). It
receives about {c_price} a course and needs {c_breakeven}; it covers its running costs once {c_be_used} of
its slots are filled. It is closer to viable than the dialysis centre, but its {c_capex} set-up cost is far
larger, and the optimistic case pays back in about {c_high_payback} years.

![Radiotherapy centre model](exports/chart6c_cancer_centre_model_scenarios.png)

> **Under the hood.** An illustrative model using public data and stated assumptions. Not investment advice.
> {k_assumed} of {k_inputs} dialysis inputs (and {c_assumed} of {c_inputs} radiotherapy inputs) are still
> assumptions to verify. The input that moves the dialysis result most is {k_driver}, which
> {k_driver_sourced}. For radiotherapy it's {c_driver}. Both models are checked against a case worked by hand.

