## 6. The honest limits

**Where is the data thin, and where do sources disagree?**

![Published national dialysis counts disagree](exports/chart4c_national_counts_disagree.png)

- **Dialysis centres:** published national counts run from {k_centres_min} to {k_centres_max}+, a
  {k_spread}-fold spread.
- **Radiotherapy machines:** published counts of *working* machines run from {c_work_min} to {c_work_max},
  against an estimated {c_need} needed ({c_need_src}). My register can name {c_named_machines} working
  machines, in {c_named_states} states; {c_states_evidence} of {n_units} states have any radiotherapy evidence
  at all.
- **Causes:** most sources don't say why a unit isn't working.
- **People:** population is modelled, not counted. Population aged 40 and over, which I wanted as a measure
  of kidney-disease risk, isn't published by state, so need uses total population. And the estimates
  disagree: GRID3/WorldPop puts Ogun at {ogun_grid3} people, more than Lagos ({lagos_grid3} people); the National
  Population Commission's {npc_year} projection has Ogun at {ogun_npc} and Lagos at {lagos_npc} people. Comparing each
  state's share of the national total, {n_pop_flags} states differ by more than {pop_flag_pct}%: {pop_flag_states}
  (GRID3 against NPC). I use GRID3 because it is one consistent model for all {n_units} units, built from satellite and
  survey data rather than projected forward from an old census, but because need is measured by population,
  this choice moves states up and down the scorecard. Ogun ranks {ogun_rank_grid3} by GRID3 population and
  {ogun_rank_npc} by the NPC projection.
- **Money:** Nigeria has no published average household income, so I use the minimum wage and poverty rates
  as yardsticks.

The spread between counts is itself a finding. Before any money moves, someone has to visit each unit and
check what works, what doesn't, and why. Public data can tell you where to look first. It can't do that check.

![Published radiotherapy counts disagree](exports/chart4f_cancer_national_counts_disagree.png)

